>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> Apple hosts in Traverse v0.15.0 can opt in to a package's signed <strong>Core ML</strong> variant. The default placement is still <code>wasm-cpu</code> inside the one shared runtime. There is no web, Android, or desktop GPU adapter.</p>

  <h2>What shipped in v0.15.0</h2>
  <p>The accelerator adapter framework is in the published release (Decision 112, <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1667">traverse#1667</a>), together with a real ONNX to Core ML converter (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1663">#1663</a>) and the Swift <code>ExactModelHost</code> Core ML path.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The host chooses, in policy.</strong> The execution policy lists placements in order. An empty list means <code>wasm-cpu</code> only.</li>
    <li><strong>A variant has to earn its turn.</strong> It runs only after an on-device self-check against its signed conformance vector, within the declared tolerance, and only if it fits the host memory ceiling. <code>require_fuel_metering</code> keeps a call on metered placements.</li>
    <li><strong>It fails closed.</strong> If nothing qualifies, the call fails with <code>placement_unavailable</code>. Every result carries placement evidence.</li>
    <li><strong>Core ML is opt-in.</strong> Swift's <code>ExactModelHost</code> gains <code>coreML</code>, variant files, and placements. Without that opt-in, the call stays on <code>wasm-cpu</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the evidence is, and what it is not</h2>
  <p>Published evidence so far is macOS <code>cargo test</code> plus the Decision 113 iPhone spike. Governed-path iPhone evidence is still open (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1630">traverse#1630</a>). This page does not quote spike timings as product performance. No host is Certified under Spec 529.</p>

  <h2>Why it is built this way</h2>
  <p>An accelerator is an implementation detail of the host, not a second runtime. The contract, the signed package, and <strong>discover → execute → trace</strong> stay the same. The adapter only runs a variant the package already signed, and the trace records which path produced the answer.</p>

  <p>This is a different thing from capability <a href="/questions/how-do-placement-targets-work.html">placement targets</a> (<code>local</code>, <code>browser</code>, and so on), which decide where a capability can be placed. Model placements decide which compute path runs a signed model inside a host.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
